\chapter{From source text to a language}
An embedded C++ DSL and an interpreted language solve different interface
problems. A C++ expression template delegates syntax checking and type checking
to the host compiler. An interpreter accepts text at runtime and must make
those decisions itself. MuDSL supports both workflows.

\section{Choose the boundaries first}
Write down the source representation, the parsed representation, and the result
representation before choosing a parser. A configuration language might parse
directly into immutable records. An optimizer benefits from a durable AST,
source locations, and explicit transformations. A language with user functions
also needs environments and a policy for retaining their captured bindings.

The useful sequence is source, tokens, syntax, validation, evaluation, and
formatting. Keep diagnostics attached to the earliest stage that can explain
the failure. An unexpected closing bracket is a syntax error; an unknown
binding is an evaluation error. Keeping this distinction makes editors and
command-line tools easier to build.

\section{An implementation exercise}
Start with arithmetic on numbers. Add precedence tests before adding variables.
Then introduce a symbol table, immutable declarations, and assignment. Finally,
add functions and lexical scopes. After each step, retain both positive and
negative examples. This progression exposes ownership and error-handling
decisions before native libraries make the implementation larger.

MuLL follows this arrangement inside the two toolkit source files: a lexer
produces positioned tokens; a precedence parser constructs syntax nodes; an
interpreter evaluates them against environments. Its public interface accepts
source strings rather than exposing those internal node classes.
